{"name":"Sweet potato / carb timing","deep":true,"image":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c0/Sweet_Potato_Snack.jpg/960px-Sweet_Potato_Snack.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sweet_Potato_Snack.jpg","caption":"Sweet Potato Snack","source":"Wikimedia Commons"},"mentions":0,"casual":{"lead":["Carb timing means eating carbohydrates such as sweet potato at set times around training, to fuel sessions and speed recovery in pursuit of more muscle.","Carbohydrates replenish muscle glycogen used in high-intensity exercise, which proponents say supports better training and recovery. A post-exercise anabolic window of about 15 to 45 minutes is described as a suggestion rather than an established fact, and sweet potato specifically has not been studied."],"sections":[{"h":"How people use it","paras":["People eat a carbohydrate meal before training, during long sessions, or soon after, with sweet potato, rice or oats as common choices. It is usually layered on top of an overall diet that already hits calorie and protein targets."],"subs":[]},{"h":"Risks & side effects","paras":["No specific risks are documented for this practice beyond general dietary considerations."],"subs":[]}]},"gallery":[{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c0/Sweet_Potato_Snack.jpg/960px-Sweet_Potato_Snack.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","full":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c0/Sweet_Potato_Snack.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=original","title":"Sweet Potato Snack","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","creator":"Louiesemendez","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sweet_Potato_Snack.jpg","source":"Wikimedia Commons"},{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/94/Sweet_Potato_Curry.jpg/960px-Sweet_Potato_Curry.jpg?utm_source=common